The controller hysteria's connection starts on, and losses the loss timer finds.

hysteria does not start a connection on BBR. quic-go gives every connection its own controller —
`congestion.NewCubicSender(..., true, // use Reno ...)`
(vendor/quic-go/internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go:153-160) — with quic-go's pacer and
HyStart, and hysteria replaces it with a new BBR only when the authentication is answered
(UseConfigured, vendor/hysteria/core/client/client.go:147-166). The handshake and the request run
on NewReno. This client ran the whole connection on BBR.

So ngtcp2_hybbr gets a second mode, quic-go's NewReno sender and pacer
(vendor/quic-go/internal/congestion/cubic_sender.go, pacer.go, hybrid_slow_start.go), entered by
ngtcp2_hybbr_start_reno before the first packet and left by the reset that installs a fresh BBR
— which the client calls on an accepted authentication. The pacer the send loop asks
(ngtcp2_hybbr_pacer_*) is quic-go's while the mode is on, hysteria's after.

And one thing both modes needed: losses declared by the loss-detection TIMER reach the controller
when they are declared, as quic-go hands them over — OnCongestionEventEx(priorInFlight, now, nil,
lost) (vendor/quic-go/internal/ackhandler/sent_packet_handler.go:937-940). ngtcp2 reports them to
the controller one by one and then says nothing, so they waited in the batch until the next ACK.

